The expression $`x + 4` has value $`7` when $`x` holds $`3`, and value $`12` when $`x` holds $`8`. The expression is the same tree in both calculations. A *state* assigns an integer to every variable name. One way to evaluate the tree is to compute its value from the leaves up. Another is to give rules for justifying a proposed value. We shall prove that a proposed value has such a justification exactly when the computation returns it.

*Sources.* Read §§1.1–1.2 and §§1.4–1.5 here. In Glynn Winskel’s _The Formal Semantics of Programming Languages_, read [§§2.1–2.2, pp. 11–16](https://web.cecs.pdx.edu/~apt/cs558/winskel2.1-5.pdf) for the syntax, state and rules, then [§3.2, Proposition 3.3, p. 30](https://www.cin.ufpe.br/~if721/intranet/TheFormalSemanticsofProgrammingLanguages.pdf) for an independent proof of uniqueness. Section 1.3 is for individual study. Return to §1.6 for assignment in Chapter 10.

# Abstract Syntax and States
%%%
tag := "ch01-s1"
%%%

*Definition 1.1 (Arithmetic expressions).* Write $`\mathbb{Z}` for the integers. Fix a set $`V` of variable names. The set $`E` of arithmetic expressions consists of the finite trees generated by the following grammar, where $`n \in {\mathbb{Z}}` and $`x \in V`.

$$`e ::= \operatorname{lit}(n)\,\ \mid \ \,\operatorname{var}(x)\,\ \mid \ \,\operatorname{add}\left( {e_{1},e_{2}} \right)`

The symbol $`::=` introduces alternatives, separated by vertical bars. A *constructor* builds a tree. The leaves $`\operatorname{lit}(n)` and $`\operatorname{var}(x)` carry an integer literal (a written constant) and a variable name, respectively. The constructor $`\operatorname{add}` joins two trees, written $`e_{1}` and $`e_{2}`, at a new root. Thus every expression has exactly one outermost constructor, and an addition has two uniquely determined, ordered subexpressions. We use $`x + 4` as a concrete notation for $`\operatorname{add}\left( {\operatorname{var}(x),\operatorname{lit}(4)} \right)`.

*Structural induction.* Let $`P` be a property of expressions. To prove that $`P` holds throughout $`E`, it suffices to prove $`P\left( {\operatorname{lit}(n)} \right)` for every $`n \in {\mathbb{Z}}` and $`P\left( {\operatorname{var}(x)} \right)` for every $`x \in V`, and to derive $`P\left( {\operatorname{add}\left( {e_{1},e_{2}} \right)} \right)` from $`P\left( e_{1} \right)` and $`P\left( e_{2} \right)` for all $`e_{1},e_{2} \in E`. This is induction on tree height. An addition has height one greater than the maximum of its children’s heights. Literals and variables have height zero.

*Definition 1.2 (States).* A state assigns exactly one integer to each variable. Write $`\Sigma = \left( {V\rightarrow{\mathbb{Z}}} \right)` for the set of all such functions and $`\sigma(x)` for the value of $`x` in state $`\sigma`. In this chapter the variable name itself identifies the component of the state to be read.

For example, let $`\sigma` return $`3` at $`x` and $`0` everywhere else. The symbol $`x` is an element of $`V`, the tree $`\operatorname{var}(x)` is an element of $`E`, and the value $`\sigma(x) = 3` is an element of $`\mathbb{Z}`. Only the last of these depends on the chosen state.

*Comparison with TAPL.* In §§3.2–3.3 Pierce builds Boolean and natural-number terms inductively. A proof about a conditional has a hypothesis for each of its three immediate subterms. Our grammar has a binary addition instead, so its induction step has two hypotheses. To recover the induction principle for a new grammar, inspect the recursive arguments of each constructor.

A constructor determines which questions may be asked of a tree. Suppose a reader hands us an expression with a plus sign at its root. We may ask for its two ordered children. If the root is a numeral, we may ask for its integer label. No test on the state can change the answers to these questions. This is why a definition by cases on expressions starts by inspecting a constructor even when a later case needs to read a state.

Here is an encoding of these two inputs in Lean. The example uses strings for the set of variable names. A store supplies a value for every string, including strings absent from a particular expression. The inductive declaration generates an induction hypothesis for each recursive field of a constructor. The integer in a literal and the name in a variable are fields too, but neither field is itself an expression.

```lean
inductive ArithmeticTree where
  | lit (n : Int)
  | var (x : String)
  | add (left right : ArithmeticTree)

abbrev IntegerStore := String → Int
```

Thus the tree for `x + x` contains two occurrences even when the two variable labels are equal. Later the recursion computing a value will visit both children. Counting variable names in a set will give a different account of the same tree.

# Evaluation
%%%
tag := "ch01-s2"
%%%

*Definition 1.3 (Evaluation).* The function $`\operatorname{eval}:E \times \Sigma\rightarrow{\mathbb{Z}}` takes a pair consisting of an expression and a state. We define it by *structural recursion*, giving one equation per constructor and using the function recursively only on its children.

$$`\begin{matrix} {\operatorname{eval}\left( {\operatorname{lit}(n),\sigma} \right)} & {= n} \\ {\operatorname{eval}\left( {\operatorname{var}(x),\sigma} \right)} & {= \sigma(x)} \\ {\operatorname{eval}\left( {\operatorname{add}\left( {e_{1},e_{2}} \right),\sigma} \right)} & {= \operatorname{eval}\left( {e_{1},\sigma} \right) + \operatorname{eval}\left( {e_{2},\sigma} \right)} \end{matrix}`

In the last clause, $`\operatorname{add}` on the left constructs an expression. The $`+` on the right adds two integers. Both recursive calls use the given state. For each fixed state, the first two clauses determine the values at height zero. The last clause determines the value of an addition from the values at smaller heights. The equations therefore determine a unique total function.

*Example 1.1.* If $`\sigma(x) = 3`, then

$$`\begin{matrix} {\operatorname{eval}\left( {\operatorname{add}\left( {\operatorname{var}(x),\operatorname{lit}(4)} \right),\sigma} \right)} & {= \operatorname{eval}\left( {\operatorname{var}(x),\sigma} \right) + \operatorname{eval}\left( {\operatorname{lit}(4),\sigma} \right)} \\ & {= 3 + 4 = 7.} \end{matrix}`

*The evaluator in Concrete Semantics.* In [_Concrete Semantics_, §§3.1.1–3.1.2, pp. 27–29](https://concrete-semantics.org/), Nipkow and Klein write N, V and Plus for our three constructors. Their function aval first takes a tree, then a state. For example, aval (Plus (V x) (N 4)) s evaluates the tree from Example 1.1 in state s. It computes s x + 4. Replacing s by a different state changes only the lookup. This is the function compared with Winskel’s rules in §1.5. [Companion theory, AExp, definition of aval.](https://isabelle.in.tum.de/library/HOL/HOL-IMP/AExp.html)

*Compositional interpretation.* In §§1.1–1.2 Reynolds interprets the constructors of predicate logic by operations on their meanings. For our arithmetic language, the meaning of an expression that may contain variables is a function from states to integers. Write $`D(e)(\sigma) = \operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\sigma} \right)`. The meaning of a literal is the constant function with value $`n`. A variable denotes the function that looks up its name. To interpret addition, take two such functions $`f,g:\Sigma\rightarrow{\mathbb{Z}}` and form their pointwise sum, the function sending $`\sigma` to $`f(\sigma) + g(\sigma)`. Then

$$`D\left( {\operatorname{add}\left( {e_{1},e_{2}} \right)} \right)(\sigma) = D\left( e_{1} \right)(\sigma) + D\left( e_{2} \right)(\sigma).`

This is precisely the third evaluation equation. It is compositional because the two functions $`D\left( e_{1} \right)` and $`D\left( e_{2} \right)` supply everything needed to interpret their sum.

As a consequence, $`\operatorname{var}(x)` and $`\operatorname{add}\left( {\operatorname{var}(x),\operatorname{lit}(0)} \right)` are distinct syntax trees with the same meaning. For every $`\sigma`, the equation reduces the second value to $`\sigma(x) + 0 = \sigma(x)`. Equality of these two meanings requires equality at every state. Agreement in a single calculation would leave open their behaviour at other states.

Fix an expression and let the state vary. The resulting map on states is constant for a literal. For a variable it is the projection that reads the named coordinate. For addition it is the pointwise sum of two maps. For example, if the values of `x` and `y` at a state are 3 and 5, the maps for `x + y` and `y + x` both give 8. To show these maps equal, replace 3 and 5 by arbitrary integers supplied by an arbitrary state and use commutativity of integer addition. The syntax trees can still be distinguished by inspecting the label of the left child.

```lean
def arithmeticValue (s : IntegerStore) : ArithmeticTree → Int
  | .lit n => n
  | .var x => s x
  | .add a b => arithmeticValue s a + arithmeticValue s b

example (s : IntegerStore) (a b : ArithmeticTree) :
    arithmeticValue s (.add a b) = arithmeticValue s (.add b a) :=
  Int.add_comm _ _
```

The proof uses the defining equation once on each side. It does not need an induction on either child: the commutative law applies to the two integers they produce, however deeply nested the children may be.

# Free Variables and the Coincidence Lemma
%%%
tag := "ch01-s3"
%%%

*Definition 1.4 (Free variables).* The function $`\operatorname{FV}:E\rightarrow\mathcal{P}(V)` assigns a set of variable names to each expression. Here $`\mathcal{P}(V)` is the set of all subsets of $`V`, $`\varnothing` is the empty set, and $`\cup` denotes set union. Define

$$`\begin{matrix} {\operatorname{FV}\left( {\operatorname{lit}(n)} \right)} & {= \varnothing} \\ {\operatorname{FV}\left( {\operatorname{var}(x)} \right)} & {= \left\{ x \right\}} \\ {\operatorname{FV}\left( {\operatorname{add}\left( {e_{1},e_{2}} \right)} \right)} & {= \operatorname{FV}\left( e_{1} \right) \cup \operatorname{FV}\left( e_{2} \right).} \end{matrix}`

An occurrence is one leaf labelled with a variable name. A *binder* introduces a local name within a specified part of a term, its scope. An occurrence is *free* if no enclosing binder binds it. Our three constructors introduce no binders, so every variable occurrence is free. Two occurrences of the same name contribute one element to the set. For $`S \subseteq V`, let $`\sigma \rvert_{S}:S\rightarrow{\mathbb{Z}}` denote the restriction of $`\sigma` to $`S`. Thus $`\sigma \rvert_{S} = \tau\rvert_{S}` holds exactly when $`\sigma(x) = \tau(x)` for every $`x \in S`.

*Lemma 1.1 (Coincidence).* For every $`e \in E` and $`\sigma,\tau \in \Sigma`,

$$`\sigma \rvert_{\operatorname{FV}{(e)}} = \tau \rvert_{\operatorname{FV}{(e)}}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\sigma} \right) = \operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\tau} \right).`

*Proof.* Proceed by structural induction on $`e`. The induction assertion for each expression quantifies over all pairs of states. Fix $`\sigma` and $`\tau` that agree on $`\operatorname{FV}(e)`.

If $`e = \operatorname{lit}(n)`, both evaluations give $`n`. If $`e = \operatorname{var}(x)`, then $`\operatorname{FV}(e) = \left\{ x \right\}`, so $`\sigma(x) = \tau(x)` gives the required equality.

If $`e = \operatorname{add}\left( {e_{1},e_{2}} \right)`, agreement on $`\operatorname{FV}\left( e_{1} \right) \cup \operatorname{FV}\left( e_{2} \right)` implies agreement on each of the two sets. By the induction hypotheses, $`\operatorname{eval}\left( {e_{i},\sigma} \right) = \operatorname{eval}\left( {e_{i},\tau} \right)` for $`i = 1,2`. Therefore

$$`\begin{matrix} {\operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\sigma} \right)} & {= \operatorname{eval}\left( {e_{1},\sigma} \right) + \operatorname{eval}\left( {e_{2},\sigma} \right)} \\ & {= \operatorname{eval}\left( {e_{1},\tau} \right) + \operatorname{eval}\left( {e_{2},\tau} \right)} \\ & {= \operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\tau} \right).} \end{matrix}`

*Corollary 1.2 (State update).* For $`\sigma \in \Sigma`, $`x \in V`, and $`n \in {\mathbb{Z}}`, define $`\sigma\left\lbrack {x\mapsto n} \right\rbrack` to have value $`n` at $`x` and to agree with $`\sigma` at every other variable. For every $`e \in E` with $`x \notin \operatorname{FV}(e)`,

$$`\operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\sigma\left\lbrack {x\mapsto n} \right\rbrack} \right) = \operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\sigma} \right).`

*Proof.* The update leaves every variable in $`\operatorname{FV}(e)` unchanged. Apply Lemma 1.1.$`\square`

An expression is *closed* if its set of free variables is empty. Any two states agree on the empty set, so a closed expression has the same value in every state by Lemma 1.1.

*Reading the Lean formulation.* In the Lean 4 port of Leroy’s course, free\_in\_aexp x a expresses the proposition that the name $`x` occurs free in the expression $`a`. The hypothesis of aeval\_free says that for every such $`x` the two stores return equal integers. Taking $`a = \operatorname{add}\left( {\operatorname{var}(x),\operatorname{var}(x)} \right)` makes that a single condition on the stores, although the evaluation equation looks up $`x` twice. The port includes subtraction, so its proof also has a subtraction case. [Leroy, Imp.lean, free variables and coincidence.](https://github.com/leanprover/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/blob/a2730e7f9228b9d7ecf4510f1f8652f2cae7ba01/LeroyCompilerVerificationCourse/Imp.lean#L52-L60)

The agreement hypothesis has a direction that is easy to reverse by accident. Take the expression `x + y` and two stores that give the pairs `(1,4)` and `(2,3)` at the two variables. Both evaluations give 5. Neither coordinate agrees. Consequently an equality of results for one pair of stores does not allow us to infer coordinatewise agreement on the free variables. The lemma instead tells us how to produce an equality of results *from* enough equalities of inputs.

A slightly stronger way to read the statement is to retain only the restriction of a store to the finite set of names in the expression. Any two extensions of this restricted assignment to all names give the same value. To see this, take the two extensions as the stores in the coincidence lemma; for a free name, equality follows because both extend the same assignment. Thus an evaluator supplied with a full store can be used to define a value from just the restricted assignment. Choosing zero for every missing variable gives one concrete extension. Choosing a different default gives the same result. This argument explains why a later data-flow analysis can reason about sets of names even though the execution rules operate on complete stores.

# Evaluation Judgments
%%%
tag := "ch01-s4"
%%%

Definition 1.3 computes a value. We can also specify which triples of state, expression, and integer constitute valid evaluations. Write $`\sigma \vdash e \Downarrow n` for the *judgment* that $`e` evaluates to $`n` in $`\sigma`. The symbol $`\Downarrow` here represents a complete evaluation. Such a relation is called a *big-step* semantics.

*Reading judgments.* The turnstile $`\vdash` separates the state supplied to a judgment from the expression or command being evaluated. In $`\sigma \vdash e \Downarrow n` the result is an integer. In $`\sigma \vdash c \Downarrow \sigma'` the result is a final state. Both assert that evaluation terminates. $`\langle c,\sigma\rangle \rightarrow \langle c',\sigma'\rangle` records one transition between configurations, each a pair of a command and its current state. The superscript in $`\rightarrow^{*}` allows any finite number of transitions, including zero. The proof that the expression judgment agrees with the function $`\operatorname{eval}(e,\sigma)` is Theorem 1.3.

*Definition 1.5 (Evaluation relation).* The relation is the least subset of $`\Sigma \times E \times {\mathbb{Z}}` closed under the following rules. A set is closed under a rule if it contains the conclusion below the line whenever it contains all premises above the line. A rule with no premises is an axiom. Here $`a,b,n` range over integers.

$$`\frac{}{\sigma \vdash \operatorname{lit}(n) \Downarrow n}\quad\frac{}{\sigma \vdash \operatorname{var}(x) \Downarrow \sigma(x)}`

$$`\frac{\sigma \vdash e_{1} \Downarrow a\quad \sigma \vdash e_{2} \Downarrow b}{\sigma \vdash \operatorname{add}\left( {e_{1},e_{2}} \right) \Downarrow a + b}`

“Least” refers to inclusion. A triple belongs to the relation exactly when there is a finite *derivation* for it, a tree whose nodes are rule conclusions and whose children supply their premises. Indeed, the triples with finite derivations are closed under the rules by adjoining a root. Every closed relation contains all such triples, by induction from the leaves to the root.

*Example 1.2.* Take a state $`\sigma` with $`\sigma(x) = 3`. The two leaves justify the premises of the addition rule.

$$`\frac{\frac{}{\sigma \vdash \operatorname{var}(x) \Downarrow 3}\quad\frac{}{\sigma \vdash \operatorname{lit}(4) \Downarrow 4}}{\sigma \vdash \operatorname{add}\left( {\operatorname{var}(x),\operatorname{lit}(4)} \right) \Downarrow 7}`

Conversely, suppose the same addition evaluates to $`n`. Only the addition rule has that outer constructor in its conclusion. Its premises require the values $`3` and $`4`, so $`n = 7`. Recovering the possible last rule and its premises from the conclusion is called *inversion*.

Closure alone would admit additional judgments. For example, the set of all triples is closed under every rule above. In that set $`\sigma \vdash \operatorname{lit}(4) \Downarrow 9` would also hold. No finite derivation can have that conclusion. Its last rule would have to be the literal axiom, whose result for this leaf is $`4`. Taking the least closed relation makes inversion valid.

*Notation in the source.* Winskel writes $`\langle a,\sigma\rangle \rightarrow n` (§2.2, p. 13) and $`\langle c,\sigma\rangle \rightarrow \sigma'` (§2.4, p. 19) for complete expression and command evaluations. Here the double downward arrow serves that role in both judgments. His addition rule makes the sum of the two premise results a side condition on the conclusion. Definition 1.5 writes that sum directly in the conclusion. [Winskel, §§2.2, 2.4](https://www.cin.ufpe.br/~if721/intranet/TheFormalSemanticsofProgrammingLanguages.pdf#page=34)

The BigStep chapter of _PLFA_ uses $`\gamma \vdash M \Downarrow V` for evaluation of a lambda term. Its $`\gamma` is an environment that binds variables to closures (terms paired with environments for their free variables). In our imperative language the object supplied to the left of the turnstile is the current store of integer variables. [PLFA, BigStep](https://plfa.github.io/BigStep/#big-step-evaluation)

The following declaration fixes the store as a parameter of an inductive family. The expression and the resulting integer are indices. Each constructor says exactly which judgment it can conclude. In particular the conclusion of the addition constructor contains the sum of its premise results. Producing an arbitrary integer would require further evidence equating it to that sum.

```lean
inductive ArithmeticDerives (s : IntegerStore) : ArithmeticTree → Int → Prop where
  | lit (n : Int) : ArithmeticDerives s (.lit n) n
  | var (x : String) : ArithmeticDerives s (.var x) (s x)
  | add {a b : ArithmeticTree} {m n : Int} :
      ArithmeticDerives s a m → ArithmeticDerives s b n →
      ArithmeticDerives s (.add a b) (m + n)
```

To build a derivation for `x + 4` at an arbitrary store, instantiate the variable rule with `x` and the literal rule with 4. The two conclusions determine both arguments to the addition on the result side. Inverting a derivation starts from the other end: if its conclusion mentions a sum at the expression index, neither leaf constructor could have produced that index. Inspecting the remaining constructor yields two particular integers together with derivations for them. Those witnesses come from the supplied derivation; there is no need to choose an algorithm for finding them.

# Correspondence and Determinacy
%%%
tag := "ch01-s5"
%%%

For fixed $`e` and $`\sigma`, Definition 1.3 supplies an integer. Definition 1.5 gives a test of what can be justified by the rules. Each candidate $`n` either has a finite derivation or has none. The following theorem identifies the set of justifiable results as the singleton containing the computed integer.

*Theorem 1.3 (Correspondence).* For every $`e \in E`, $`\sigma \in \Sigma`, and $`n \in {\mathbb{Z}}`,

$$`\sigma \vdash e \Downarrow n\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\sigma} \right) = n.`

*Proof (from a derivation to a value).* Induct on the given derivation. This allows the assertion for each immediate premise to be used in proving the assertion for the conclusion. In the literal and variable cases the conclusion agrees with the corresponding equation of Definition 1.3. In the addition case the premises have results $`a` and $`b`, and the conclusion has result $`a + b`. The two induction hypotheses give $`\operatorname{eval}\left( {e_{1},\sigma} \right) = a` and $`\operatorname{eval}\left( {e_{2},\sigma} \right) = b`. The equation for addition now gives the result $`a + b`.

*Proof (from a value to a derivation).* Fix a state $`\sigma`. By structural induction on $`e` we construct a derivation with result $`\operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\sigma} \right)`. For a literal use the literal axiom. For a variable use the variable axiom. For an addition, the induction hypotheses provide both premise derivations. Adjoin the addition rule at the root. Its result is $`\operatorname{eval}\left( {e_{1},\sigma} \right) + \operatorname{eval}\left( {e_{2},\sigma} \right)`, which equals $`\operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\sigma} \right)`. If $`\operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\sigma} \right) = n`, this is the required derivation.$`\square`

*Corollary 1.4.* Evaluation is *total*, meaning each expression and state have a result, and *deterministic*, meaning they have at most one result.

*Proof.* Existence follows by constructing the derivation for $`\operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\sigma} \right)`. If two derivations yield $`n` and $`m` for the same inputs, Theorem 1.3 gives $`n = \operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\sigma} \right) = m`.$`\square`

The forward proof begins with evidence of evaluation and examines its last rule. The reverse proof begins with syntax and constructs that evidence. In this language an addition has exactly one premise per child. For a command that repeats its body, one premise may concern the very same command in a new state. It is then a smaller premise derivation even though the command itself has the same syntax.

*A direct proof of determinacy.* In §3.2, Proposition 3.3, Winskel proves uniqueness directly from the rules by structural induction on the expression. Here is the addition case for our grammar. Fix $`e = \operatorname{add}\left( {e_{1},e_{2}} \right)` and suppose two derivations have results $`r` and $`s` in the same state. Inverting their last rules produces child results $`a,b` and $`a',b'`, with $`r = a + b` and $`s = a' + b'`. The induction hypothesis for $`e_{1}` applies to its two evaluations in this state, so $`a = a'`. The hypothesis for $`e_{2}` gives $`b = b'`. Hence $`r = s`. The axiom cases force the results to be the literal or the value stored at the given name. This proof establishes uniqueness even before an evaluator has been defined. Existence is a separate assertion, supplied here by the reverse direction of Theorem 1.3.

In the forward direction the data in hand include an entire derivation. In its addition case Lean gives an equality for each child result. Rewriting with those equalities changes a sum of two computed values into the declared result of the addition rule. In the reverse direction the input is the tree. Each child induction hypothesis must now supply evidence on the judgment side. The addition constructor consumes precisely those two pieces of evidence.

```lean
theorem arithmeticSound (s : IntegerStore) {e : ArithmeticTree} {n : Int}
    (d : ArithmeticDerives s e n) : arithmeticValue s e = n := by
  induction d with
  | lit n => rfl
  | var x => rfl
  | add h₁ h₂ ih₁ ih₂ => simp only [arithmeticValue, ih₁, ih₂]

theorem arithmeticComplete (s : IntegerStore) (e : ArithmeticTree) :
    ArithmeticDerives s e (arithmeticValue s e) := by
  induction e with
  | lit n => exact .lit n
  | var x => exact .var x
  | add a b ih₁ ih₂ => exact .add ih₁ ih₂

example (s : IntegerStore) (e : ArithmeticTree) (n : Int) :
    ArithmeticDerives s e n ↔ arithmeticValue s e = n := by
  constructor
  · exact arithmeticSound s
  · intro h
    rw [← h]
    exact arithmeticComplete s e
```

The last rewriting step matters. The induction on syntax produces a derivation whose result is *the computed integer*. The theorem was stated for *any* candidate integer. The given equality identifies the candidate with the constructed index. This is where the result of the computation enters the reverse direction.

# Substitution
%%%
tag := "ch01-s6"
%%%

*Definition 1.6 (Substitution).* For $`e,t \in E` and $`x \in V`, write $`e\left\lbrack {t/x} \right\rbrack` for the tree obtained by replacing each occurrence of $`x` in $`e` with $`t`. The replacement is simultaneous. Variable occurrences inside the inserted tree are left as they were.

$$`\begin{matrix} {\operatorname{lit}(n)\left\lbrack {t/x} \right\rbrack} & {= \operatorname{lit}(n)} \\ {\operatorname{var}(y)\left\lbrack {t/x} \right\rbrack} & {= \left\{ \begin{matrix} {t\ } & {\text{if}\ y = x} \\ {\operatorname{var}(y)\ } & {\text{if}\ y \neq x} \end{matrix} \right.} \\ {\operatorname{add}\left( {e_{1},e_{2}} \right)\left\lbrack {t/x} \right\rbrack} & {= \operatorname{add}\left( {e_{1}\left\lbrack {t/x} \right\rbrack,e_{2}\left\lbrack {t/x} \right\rbrack} \right).} \end{matrix}`

*Example 1.3.* Substitute $`x + 1` for $`x` in $`x + x`. The resulting expression is $`\left( {x + 1} \right) + \left( {x + 1} \right)`. If $`\sigma(x) = 2`, it evaluates to $`6`. Evaluating the original $`x + x` in the updated state $`\sigma\left\lbrack {x\mapsto 3} \right\rbrack` gives the same result. The value assigned to $`x` by this update is computed in the original state.

*Theorem 1.5 (Substitution lemma).* For $`e,t \in E`, $`x \in V` and $`\sigma \in \Sigma`,

$$`\operatorname{eval}\left( {e\left\lbrack {t/x} \right\rbrack,\sigma} \right) = \operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\sigma\left\lbrack {x\mapsto\operatorname{eval}\left( {t,\sigma} \right)} \right\rbrack} \right).`

*Proof.* Set $`u = \operatorname{eval}\left( {t,\sigma} \right)` and $`\tau = \sigma\left\lbrack {x\mapsto u} \right\rbrack`. Use structural induction on $`e`. For a literal, both sides give its integer. For $`\operatorname{var}(y)` with $`y = x`, the left side evaluates the inserted tree to $`u` and the right side reads $`\tau(x) = u`. When $`y \neq x`, the original leaf remains, and $`\tau(y) = \sigma(y)`. For an addition, the definitions of substitution and evaluation give

$$`\begin{matrix} {\operatorname{eval}\left( {\operatorname{add}\left( {e_{1},e_{2}} \right)\left\lbrack {t/x} \right\rbrack,\sigma} \right)} & {= \operatorname{eval}\left( {e_{1}\left\lbrack {t/x} \right\rbrack,\sigma} \right) + \operatorname{eval}\left( {e_{2}\left\lbrack {t/x} \right\rbrack,\sigma} \right)} \\ & {= \operatorname{eval}\left( {e_{1},\tau} \right) + \operatorname{eval}\left( {e_{2},\tau} \right)} \\ & {= \operatorname{eval}\left( {\operatorname{add}\left( {e_{1},e_{2}} \right),\tau} \right).} \end{matrix}`

The middle equality uses the two induction hypotheses.$`\square`

In an imperative language an assignment to $`x` changes the state in which subsequent expressions are evaluated. The lemma gives a way to calculate their values in the original state by substituting the right-hand side of the assignment.

*Substitution under a binder.* Reynolds §1.4 treats languages with binding. For example, let $`P` be a predicate of two integers and consider the formula $`\forall y,P\left( {x,y} \right)` with $`x \neq y`. Substituting the free variable $`y` for $`x` should preserve the inserted variable’s dependence on the surrounding state. A literal replacement gives $`\forall y,P\left( {y,y} \right)`, where the quantifier captures it. Choose a fresh name $`z`, rename the bound variable, and then substitute to get $`\forall z,P\left( {y,z} \right)`. Definition 1.6 needs only three clauses because none of our arithmetic constructors binds a name.

Successive substitutions can expose a dependency between two assignments. Let the expression to observe be `x + y`, with distinct names, and consider the instructions `x := y; y := 1`. Starting from a state giving `x = 9` and `y = 4`, the final expression has value 5. To express the observation in the original state, eliminate the *later* assignment first. Replacing `y` by 1 in the observed expression gives `x + 1`. Now eliminate the earlier assignment by replacing `x` by `y`, giving `y + 1`. Its value at the original state is indeed 5.

Doing the two textual replacements in program order would first give `y + y` and then `1 + 1`. The loss of the old lookup of `y` identifies the error. The instructions update stores in their execution order. An observation of the final store is transported in the opposite direction, from the final observation back across each update. Each transport step is one application of the substitution lemma. This same calculation will reappear for a Boolean observation when a postcondition is moved back across a sequence of assignments.

# Exercises
%%%
tag := "ch01-s7"
number := false
%%%

*1.1 (Evaluation).* Let $`e = \operatorname{add}\left( {\operatorname{var}(x),\operatorname{add}\left( {\operatorname{lit}(4),\operatorname{var}(z)} \right)} \right)`, with $`x,y,z` distinct. Suppose $`\sigma(x) = 2`, $`\sigma(y) = 9`, and $`\sigma(z) = 5`. Compute $`\operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\sigma} \right)` and $`\operatorname{FV}(e)` using their defining equations. Use Corollary 1.2 to determine $`\operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\sigma\left\lbrack {y\mapsto - 1} \right\rbrack} \right)`.

*1.2 (Coincidence).* Let $`e = \operatorname{add}\left( {\operatorname{var}(x),\operatorname{var}(y)} \right)`, with $`x \neq y`.

1. Give two states that agree at $`x` and yield different values for $`e`.
2. Give two states that disagree at both $`x` and $`y` and yield the same value for $`e`. State the converse of Lemma 1.1 and explain why this example refutes it.

*1.3 (Variable occurrences).* Write $`{\mathbb{N}} = \left\{ {0,1,2,\ldots} \right\}` for the natural numbers. For each $`x \in V`, define $`m_{x}:E\rightarrow{\mathbb{N}}` by structural recursion so that $`m_{x}(e)` counts the occurrences of $`x` in $`e`. Prove by structural induction that for every $`e \in E`, every $`\sigma \in \Sigma`, and every $`k \in {\mathbb{Z}}`,

$$`\operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\sigma\left\lbrack {x\mapsto\sigma(x) + k} \right\rbrack} \right) = \operatorname{eval}\left( {e,\sigma} \right) + k \cdot m_{x}(e).`

Call $`S \subseteq V` sufficient for $`e` if agreement on $`S` implies equal evaluations of $`e`. Use Lemma 1.1 and the equation with $`k = 1` to show that $`\operatorname{FV}(e)` is the least sufficient set under inclusion.

*1.4 (Derivations).* Extend the grammar with $`\operatorname{neg}(e)` for integer negation. Add one equation to Definition 1.3 and one rule to Definition 1.5. Prove the new case in each direction of Theorem 1.3. Write down exactly what your induction hypothesis supplies in each proof.

*1.5 (Substitution).* For distinct names $`x` and $`y`, take $`e = x + y` and $`t = y + 1`. Compare $`e\left\lbrack {t/x} \right\rbrack` and $`e\left\lbrack {x/y} \right\rbrack` in a state with $`\sigma(x) = 4` and $`\sigma(y) = 2`. For each substitution identify the single state update that yields the same result on the original tree.

*Further problem.* Extend the arithmetic language with subtraction over the integers. Say that a name is essential to an expression if two states differing only at that name can give different values. Does membership in the set of free variables characterize essential names? Give an exact decision procedure for the extended language and justify both directions.

# Seminar Worksheet
